---
description: Higgsfield asset workflow and documentation requirements
alwaysApply: true
---

- When generating game art, check for the Higgsfield MCP connection first and
  prefer it. Current status: it WAS available and used for this slice
  (model `nano_banana_pro`); see `docs/HIGGSFIELD_PROMPTS.md`.
- Every prompt includes the full game context, mechanic trigger, player meaning, source/target,
  shot/framing, mobile readability and either the excursion or casino-reward suffix from
  `docs/context/ANIMATION_AND_ASSETS.md`.
- Default art direction is a warm cartoon pirate excursion. Use neon Coin Master/slot-machine
  energy for loot-box/jackpot rewards and specifically approved high-stakes peaks only.
- Ask for a "plain solid dark background for easy cutout" (models cannot emit
  alpha), then run `python3 scripts/chroma_key.py <file>` to produce the
  transparent `-cut` version. Keep raw generations in
  `client/src/assets/higgsfield/loot-drop/raw/`.
- Register each used file in the active React asset map/component and the appropriate
  `client/src/assets/higgsfield/**/asset-manifest.json`, with job ID, exact prompt, model,
  source/runtime paths, usage/reason and fallback. Do not add production registrations only to
  archived `experiments/loot-drop/` manifests.
- Never pretend Higgsfield was used when it wasn't. If it is unavailable or
  fails, proceed with procedural art and add exact ready-to-run prompts to
  `docs/HIGGSFIELD_ASSET_TODO.md`.
- Higgsfield-authored sprites/keyframes are preferred over baked video when game state, target,
  colour or timing is dynamic. Baked video must never contain secret/variable game data.
- Higgsfield art never replaces the fallback system — the game must still boot when optional
  binary assets fail.
